  "generated_text": "To solve for the value of \\( k \\), we need to determine the radii of both circles and use the given information about the points on the circles.\n\nFirst, consider the larger circle. The point \\( P(8, 6) \\) lies on this circle. The distance from the origin \\( O \\) to \\( P \\) is the radius of the larger circle. We calculate this distance using the distance formula:\n\\[\nOP = \\sqrt{8^2 + 6^2} = \\sqrt{64 + 36} = \\sqrt{100} = 10\n\\]\nThus, the radius of the larger circle is 10.\n\nNext, consider the smaller circle. The point \\( S(0, k) \\) lies on this circle. The distance from the origin \\( O \\) to \\( S \\) is the radius of the smaller circle. We calculate this distance:\n\\[\nOS = \\sqrt{0^2 + k^2} = |k|\n\\]\nSince \\( S \\) is on the circle, the radius of the smaller circle is \\( |k| \\).\n\nWe are given that the distance \\( QR = 3 \\). The points \\( Q \\) and \\( R \\) are the points where the larger circle intersects the \\( x \\)-axis. The coordinates of \\( Q \\) are \\( (7, 0) \\) and the coordinates of \\( R \\) are \\( (10, 0) \\). The distance \\( QR \\) is:\n\\[\nQR = 10 - 7 = 3\n\\]\nThis confirms that the radius of the larger circle is indeed 10.\n\nSince \\( S \\) is on the smaller circle and the distance from the origin to \\( S \\) is \\( |k| \\), and we know that \\( S \\) must be within the radius of the smaller circle, we have:\n\\[\n|k| = 7\n\\]\nThus, \\( k \\) can be either 7 or -7. However, since \\( S \\) is above the \\( x \\)-axis (as indicated by the problem setup), \\( k \\) must be positive. Therefore, \\( k = 7 \\).\n\nThe value of \\( k \\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
